# Module 2: Embedded Hardware Architectures

**Level:** Intermediate · **Time:** ~60 min · **Hardware:** NUCLEO-F411RE

**Prerequisites:** Module 1, C pointers and bitwise operators, binary/hex arithmetic.

## Learning Objectives

By the end of this module you will be able to:

1. Distinguish MPUs from MCUs structurally and justify the choice for a given product.
2. Explain Von Neumann vs. Harvard and CISC vs. RISC, and say where real MCUs actually sit.
3. Describe how code, data, stack and peripherals are laid out in memory, and access peripheral registers through the memory map.

---

## 1. Microprocessors (MPUs) vs. Microcontrollers (MCUs)

### 1.1 Structural difference

- An **MPU** is essentially the CPU (plus MMU and caches). Main memory (DRAM), storage and most peripherals live **outside** the chip, on the board.
- An **MCU** integrates the CPU, **flash**, **SRAM**, clocks, reset logic and **peripherals** (GPIO, timers, UART, SPI, I2C, ADC) on **one die**.

| | MCU (e.g. STM32F411) | MPU / application processor (e.g. Cortex-A SoC) |
|---|---|---|
| Memory | On-chip flash + SRAM (KB to low MB) | External DRAM + external storage (GB) |
| MMU | Usually none (optional MPU for protection) | Yes, virtual memory |
| Software | Bare-metal / RTOS | Linux / Android |
| Boot time | Microseconds to milliseconds | Seconds |
| Timing determinism | High | Lower |
| Board complexity | Low (few passives, often 2 layers) | High (DRAM routing, power sequencing, 4+ layers) |
| Power | µA to mA | mW to W |

### 1.2 Why the structure matters (link to Module 1)

- **Cost and size:** integration removes parts and board area.
- **Power:** no external DRAM to refresh, so deep sleep is possible at µA levels.
- **Reliability:** fewer components and interconnects mean fewer failure points.
- **Trade-off:** you give up the MMU, virtual memory and the large software ecosystem.

> **Rule of thumb:** if the product needs a full OS, a filesystem, a display stack or heavy networking, look at an MPU. If it needs tight timing, low power and a small BOM, look at an MCU.

