## 2. Von Neumann vs. Harvard

### 2.1 The two models

| | Von Neumann | Harvard |
|---|---|---|
| Code and data | Same memory, same bus | Separate memories and buses |
| Fetch + data access | Sequential (the *Von Neumann bottleneck*) | Can happen in parallel |
| Address space | Single | Often separate (code vs. data) |
| Pros | Simple, flexible | Higher throughput, code can't be overwritten by data |
| Examples | Classic PCs, Cortex-M0/M0+ | AVR (ATmega), 8051, many DSPs |

### 2.2 Reality: modified Harvard

Most modern MCUs and CPUs blend both:

- **Cortex-M3/M4** (e.g. STM32F411) has **separate internal buses** for instruction fetch, data access and system peripherals, so fetches and data accesses can overlap. The programmer still sees **one unified address space**.
- **AVR** is "true" Harvard: separate address spaces mean constants in flash need special access (e.g. `PROGMEM`).
- **x86 PCs** are Von Neumann at the memory level but use split L1 instruction/data caches, which is Harvard-like inside the CPU.

> **Practical consequence:** on a unified address space (Cortex-M) a pointer reaches flash or RAM with the same instructions. On a split-space architecture (AVR) it does not, and the compiler needs to be told which space a pointer refers to.

