## 3. CISC vs. RISC

| | CISC | RISC |
|---|---|---|
| Instruction set | Large, many specialised instructions | Small, simple instructions |
| Instruction length | Variable | Mostly fixed (or a small set of sizes) |
| Memory access | Many instructions can operate directly on memory | **Load/store**: only load and store touch memory |
| Hardware | Complex decode (x86 translates to internal micro-ops) | Simpler decode, easier to pipeline |
| Code density | Often higher | Historically lower, mitigated by compressed encodings |
| Examples | x86, 8051 | ARM, RISC-V, AVR, MIPS |

Notes for embedded work:

- **Cortex-M** uses the **Thumb-2** instruction set: a mix of 16- and 32-bit encodings that keeps code small while retaining load/store RISC behavior.
- The distinction has **blurred**: modern x86 decodes to RISC-like micro-ops, and RISC cores add specialised instructions (DSP, FPU).
- For firmware, what matters more than the label is **code density (flash cost)**, **energy per operation** and **toolchain/ecosystem support**.

