## 4. Memory Organization

### 4.1 Memory types in an MCU

| Memory | Volatile? | Holds | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Flash | No | Code (`.text`), constants (`.rodata`), initial values of `.data` | Slower than RAM, limited erase cycles, wait states at high clock |
| SRAM | Yes | Variables, stack, heap | Fast, small, power-hungry compared to flash at rest |
| Peripheral registers | n/a | Control/status of hardware | Not memory: reads and writes have side effects |
| EEPROM / external | No | Configuration, logs | Not present on every MCU |

### 4.2 Program sections

| Section | Contents | Lives in (at rest) | Runtime location |
|---|---|---|---|
| `.text` | Machine code | Flash | Flash |
| `.rodata` | `const` data, strings | Flash | Flash |
| `.data` | Initialised globals | Flash (initial values) | **Copied to RAM** at startup |
| `.bss` | Zero-initialised globals | Not stored | **Zeroed in RAM** at startup |
| Heap | Dynamic allocation | n/a | RAM, grows upward |
| Stack | Call frames, locals | n/a | RAM, grows **downward** on Cortex-M |

The startup code (run before `main()`) copies `.data` from flash to RAM and clears `.bss`. This is why an initialised global costs both flash *and* RAM.

### 4.3 Boot on a Cortex-M

At reset the core reads the **vector table**:

1. Word 0: initial **stack pointer** value.
2. Word 1: address of the **Reset handler**, where execution begins.

